Well, like I said, many-worlds and whatever else I'm jabbering about
are not the same idea.  On the other hand they seem similar.

In Many Worlds, probability is a relation between a starting point
and an ending point.  Bunches of ending points all exist but they have
different probabilities from the point of view of the starting point.
I may have it wrong but I didn't just make it up.  I also admit I have a
hard time understanding what it means or how it works.

Also, I admit I have a stretched idea of MW because the idea of the initial
wave equation seems redundant given the effects of point of view.  (The
initial wave equation represents what you knew at the beginning, while
worlds represent points of view with further knowledge.)  Everything
might as well be branches off a great everything-is-possible, as far as
I can see it mattering to the theory.  That would make *every* conceivable
world (within our laws of physics) exist, but all having different probability
relations between them.  I think.  But I don't think that's much weirder
than regular MW, where there's just a smaller set of co-existing worlds.

There's a point in Everett's original paper where he says that *IF* there
has to be some measure of worlds that's like probability (i.e., something
that's always conserved, always between 0 and 1, where specific events are
always less probable than general events that include them, etc.), then the
only number it makes sense to assign to it is the ol'
absolute-value-of-the- normalized-amplitude-squared that shows up in QM
experiments.

That's bizarre, but to take off from there, maybe probability is part
of the *definition* of experience, so that abstract patterns or truths
can map to experiences only according to how they map to the laws of
probability.

(And besides, it's *less* bizarre than the other variation on MW that says
something about things dissipating heat until they settle into QM-like
probability distributions!)

